<p>
    The Code 39 barcode is a widely used linear barcode symbology that can encode alphanumeric characters and a limited set of special characters.
    It is commonly used in various industries for inventory management, asset tracking, and identification purposes.
    You can use the <code>Code39Symbology</code> component to generate Code 39 barcodes in your application. It does not have any specific parameters to configure, but it supports encoding of uppercase letters (A-Z), numbers (0-9), and a few special characters (-, ., $, /, +, %).
    In this symbology, you can enable the checksum, which is an optional feature that adds an additional character to the end of the barcode to help ensure data integrity during scanning.
    The checksum is calculated based on the values of the characters in the barcode and can help detect errors that may occur during scanning, such as misreads or damaged barcodes.
    When enabled, the checksum character is typically represented as an asterisk (*) at the start and the end of the barcode.

    You can enable the checksum by setting the <code>Checksum</code> parameter to true in the <code>Code39Symbology</code> component.
    You can use the extended code39 to encode all the ASCII characters by setting the <code>IsExtended</code> parameter to true.
    You can change the height and width of the bars by setting the <code>ModuleHeight</code> and <code>ModuleWidth</code> parameters respectively.
</p>


<div style="height: 400px">
    <FluentStack Height="100%" VerticalGap="50" Orientation="Orientation.Vertical">
        <FluentStack HorizontalGap="8px">
            <FluentTextInput Immediate
                             Label="Value to encode as barcode"
                             @bind-Value="Value" />
        </FluentStack>
        
        <FluentCxBarcode Value="@Value">
            <Code39Symbology />
        </FluentCxBarcode>
    </FluentStack>
</div>

@code {
    private string Value { get; set; } = string.Empty;
}
